### 4.3 Higher harmonics

Higher harmonics of the momentum anisotropy are expected to be small [87, 88]. It was pointed out however that at higher transverse momenta these higher coefficients may become significant. Moreover the higher harmonics could be more sensitive to the initial conditions of the system than the elliptic flow coefficient \(v_{2}\)[89]. In addition it was shown that they depend on the equation of state [90]. In recent work [91, 92], \(v_{4}/v_{2}^{2}\) is proposed as a detailed probe of ideal hydrodynamic behavior. In ideal hydrodynamics, the ratio approaches to 0.5. Deviations from ideal-fluid behavior yield higher values due to the increased value of \(v_{4}\) and this ratio is directly related to the degree of thermalization of the medium [91, 92].

It has been found by STAR in Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}=200\) GeV that the integrated \(v_{4}\) is indeed an order of magnitude smaller than \(v_{2}\) as expected (see Fig. 4.31 (a)). This figure also shows that the higher harmonic \(v_{6}\) is consistent with zero. For the differential flow, shown in Fig. 4.31 (b), \(v_{4}\) indeed becomes significant at higher \(p_{t}\) as expected. It was also found that these higher harmonics scale as \(v_{2}^{n/2}\). The observed ratio of \(v_{4}/v_{2}^{2}\) is larger than unity which is in disagreement with the value 0.5 expected from ideal fluid behavior.

#### Transverse momentum dependence

With the large data set in Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{NN}}}=200\) GeV, the fourth harmonic coefficient can be measured more differentially and up to higher transverse momenta.

